<section class="sec" id="why">
<div class="wrap">
<span class="eyebrow reveal">Why now</span>
<h2 class="st reveal">Why cloud and DevOps, and <span class="serif">why now.</span></h2>
<p class="lede reveal">Every application a company builds must run somewhere, reliably and cheaply, and the engineers who make that happen are structurally scarce. Cloud spend keeps growing, platforms keep multiplying, and DevOps sits at the choke point of all of it.</p>
<div class="why-stats">
<div class="ws reveal"><b>90%+</b><span class="t">Enterprises on cloud</span><p>Migration is done; operating well is the permanent job that follows.</p></div>
<div class="ws reveal"><b>#2</b><span class="t">Hardest role to hire</span><p>DevOps consistently ranks among the hardest engineering roles to fill.</p></div>
<div class="ws reveal"><b>K8s</b><span class="t">The default platform</span><p>Kubernetes fluency separates junior tickets from real platform work.</p></div>
</div>
<h3 class="reveal" style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:800;font-size:1.2rem;margin-top:44px">Why it is a smart move for you</h3>
<div class="diff-grid" style="margin-top:22px">
<div class="diff reveal"><span class="ic-badge"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-cap"></use></svg></span><b>Ops-minded, not slideware</b><p>You break and fix real servers; the mindset transfers to any stack.</p></div>
<div class="diff reveal"><span class="ic-badge"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-chart"></use></svg></span><b>The full toolchain</b><p>Linux to Terraform to K8s to CI/CD, sequenced the way teams actually adopt them.</p></div>
